The pixel-measurement version is the right next move, and I want to be precise about what it can and can't produce, because that determines whether it's worth doing.
What it can produce: If published drawings and photographs of Göbekli's Pillars 18 and 31, and Sayburç's bench-relief pillars, are at consistent scale and resolution, you can measure shoulder-to-elbow, elbow-to-belt, belt-to-base as pixel ratios. You don't need the meshes. You need front-facing images where the anthropomorphic features are clearly visible and the full pillar height is in frame. Both sites have that in their official publications.
What it can't produce: An answer to whether the same ratio holds. Two sites isn't a sample. If Göbekli and Sayburç match, you've shown consistency between two of three sites — which is suggestive but not "shared standard across the tradition." Karahan's the missing leg, and Karahan is exactly the site where the full package isn't confirmed. So the test can pass and still leave the central question open.
What the test would actually demonstrate if it passes: That the internal grammar of the anthropomorphic package is stable across two sites whose pillar dimensions vary wildly — 5.5 meters at Göbekli versus roughly 2 at Sayburç. That would be a genuinely interesting result. It would mean the convention isn't "build the pillar this big." It's "put the arms here, the belt here, the pelt here, regardless of how big the pillar is." Absolute scale varies. Relative anatomy doesn't. That's a compositional rule, not a manufacturing spec — which is exactly what a shared-but-adaptable system would look like.
The failure mode to watch for: Pixel measurements from photographs are sensitive to camera angle, lens distortion, and whether the photo is head-on or at an angle. Two measurements from two different publications shot with two different cameras can produce a false "match" or a false "mismatch" purely from perspective. So the test would need to use orthographic drawings where available, not photographs, and flag where it's using one versus the other. That's the kind of methodological discipline the room's been enforcing all night.
Honest state right now: I can't pull the specific image files from the DAI publications and run the measurements from this seat — the search layer returns text and descriptions, not the underlying figures. But the test is designed, the data sources are identified, and the methodology is defined. It's shippable to whoever has the images open.
